QUESTION 2: HOLIDAY SERVICE (Low-Latency C++ SDK & Aggregator)
================================================================================
Stage 01: Clarify
- Functional Reqs: Return holiday list (date, name, observed status); historical & future years.
- Non-Functional Reqs: End-to-end client latency < 5ms; 99.99% availability; graceful fallback.
- Out of Scope: Personal calendars, dynamic labor laws.

Stage 02: Interactions (APIs)
- Client SDK Interface: std::vector<Holiday> getData(const std::string& countryCode, int year);
- Service REST API: GET /v1/holidays?country={countryCode}&year={year}
- Response: 200 OK: {"country": "US", "year": 2027, "holidays": [{"date": "2027-01-01", "name": "New Year's Day"}]}

Stage 03: Data Model
- holidays: id (PK), country_code (CHAR(2)), year (INT), holiday_date (DATE),
  name (VARCHAR), is_observed (BOOL), last_updated (TIMESTAMP)
- Unique Index: (country_code, year, holiday_date)

Stage 04: Simple Design
- Client App -> C++ Adapter Library -> Holiday Service -> Upstream External API

Stage 05: Challenge & Improve
- Multi-Tier Caching Architecture:
  * Tier 1 (Client-Side C++ In-Memory Cache): Process RAM inside C++ library
    (LRU Cache with 24-hr TTL). Latency: < 10 microseconds.
  * Tier 2 (Distributed Redis Cache): Shared across all Holiday Service instances.
- Precomputation / Background Sync: Holidays change rarely; daily cron worker pre-fetches
  and warms Redis cache for (current_year, current_year + 1).
- Resilience: If upstream service fails, return stale cache data with degraded fallback header.

